          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "How often does guidance describe checking thyroid function once treatment is stable?",
          "answer": "**Short answer:** For someone with hypothyroidism on a stable dose of levothyroxine, the retrieved guidance says thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) testing moves to every six to twelve months once the level is steady on an unchanged dose. The source is the Endocrinology and thyroid association guidance on hypothyroidism testing and monitoring.\n\n**Before stability:** The same guidance says TSH is rechecked six to eight weeks after starting levothyroxine or after any dose change. Testing sooner, at two or three weeks, still reflects the old state and can lead to unnecessary dose changes.\n\n**Where authorities differ:** The retrieved passages do not show a second authority giving a different interval for stable hypothyroidism. The disagreement flagged in that guidance concerns whether to treat mild (subclinical) hypothyroidism, not how often to test. I have not filled this gap from memory, so I cannot tell you whether other bodies recommend something different.\n\n**Gaps:**\n- I found no retrieved guidance on monitoring after stable treatment for an overactive thyroid (hyperthyroidism), so I cannot answer that part.\n- The passages give the interval but do not state how strong the evidence behind it is.\n\nA clinician can confirm the right interval for a specific situation, especially if the dose or other medicines have changed.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
